           <video src={assetPath('videos/intro.mp4')} controls class="video-player"></video>
        
        2. External video link (YouTube, etc.): Use iframe, e.g.:
           <iframe src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/VIDEO_ID" frameborder="0" allowfullscreen class="video-player"></iframe>
        
        3. Currently displaying placeholder, uncomment and replace the code above to use video
      -->
      <div class="video-placeholder">
        <div class="video-placeholder-icon">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <polygon points="5 3 19 12 5 21 5 3"></polygon>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <p class="video-placeholder-text">Video Placeholder - Please add video file or video link</p>
      </div>
      <!-- 
        Uncomment the code below and modify the path/link to use video:
        <video src={assetPath('videos/intro.mp4')} controls class="video-player"></video>
        Or use iframe:
        <iframe src="YOUR_VIDEO_URL" frameborder="0" allowfullscreen class="video-player"></iframe>
      -->
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="hero-qa">
    <div class="qa-item">
      <h3 class="qa-question">Why Theory of Space?</h3>
      <p class="qa-answer">
        Most evaluations of foundation models measure passive spatial reasoning from fixed observations or scripted trajectories. This misses a core requirement of embodied intelligence. Under partial observability, an agent must decide what to observe next to reduce uncertainty and build a globally consistent spatial belief. Theory of Space fills this gap by evaluating spatial belief construction as an active loop of exploration, belief formation, and belief utilization, rather than only downstream answers.
      </p>
      <div class="qa-figure">
        <div class="qa-image-wrapper">
          <img
            src={assetPath('images/tos_first.png')}
            alt="Theory of Space: active exploration, probed belief, and evaluation"
            class="qa-image"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </div>
        <p class="qa-caption">THEORY OF SPACE: active exploration, probed belief, and evaluation.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="qa-item">
      <h3 class="qa-question">What is the challenge:</h3>
      <p class="qa-answer">
        The challenge is active decision-making under partial observability. The agent must decide what to observe next to reduce uncertainty and integrate egocentric observations into a globally consistent spatial belief.
      </p>
      <div class="qa-figure">
        <div class="qa-image-wrapper">
          <img
            src={assetPath('active_exploration_example.png')}
            alt="Active exploration under partial observability"
            class="qa-image"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </div>
        <p class="qa-caption">Active exploration under partial observability: belief is built by choosing informative views to reduce uncertainty.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="qa-answer">
        This requires the agent to:
      </p>
      <ul class="qa-list">
        <li>Identify what is unknown in the current belief</li>
        <li>Choose actions that reduce uncertainty efficiently</li>
        <li>Stop when uncertainty is sufficiently reduced</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="qa-item">
      <h3 class="qa-question">What do we do?</h3>
      <p class="qa-answer">
        We implement Theory of Space in parallel text-based and vision-based environments under partial observability, allowing controlled comparisons across modalities.
      </p>
      
      <!-- Block 1: Task Suite -->
      <div class="qa-block">
        <div class="qa-block-content">
          <ul class="qa-block-list">
            <li>We design a spatial task suite to evaluate belief utilization at two levels: route-level (egocentric) and survey-level (allocentric). Additionally, we include belief updating via False Belief under covert environment changes.</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="qa-figure">
            <div class="qa-image-wrapper">
              <img
                src={assetPath('tos_eval.png')}
                alt="Task suite for Theory of Space"
                class="qa-image"
                loading="lazy"
              />
            </div>
            <p class="qa-caption">Task suite for Theory of Space covering route-level egocentric belief and survey-level allocentric belief.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      
      <!-- Block 2: Active-Passive Protocols -->
      <div class="qa-block">
        <div class="qa-block-content">
          <ul class="qa-block-list">
            <li>We evaluate models under two complementary protocols:</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="qa-protocols-grid">
            <div class="qa-protocol-item">
              <h4 class="qa-protocol-title">Active Exploration</h4>
              <p class="qa-protocol-description">Where the agent autonomously selects actions to gather informative observations under partial observability.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="qa-protocol-item">
              <h4 class="qa-protocol-title">Passive Comprehension</h4>
              <p class="qa-protocol-description">Where the model reasons from standardized observation logs generated by scripted proxy explorers to disentangle exploration strategy from spatial reasoning.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      
      <!-- Block 3: Probing Internal Belief -->
      <div class="qa-block">
        <div class="qa-block-content">
          <ul class="qa-block-list">
            <li>Beyond downstream accuracy, we explicitly probe the model's internal spatial belief by requiring it to externalize a cognitive map and uncertainty at each step. The belief map is generated from the exploration history and updated as new observations arrive, enabling direct evaluation of belief quality and diagnostic analysis beyond final answers.</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="qa-figure">
            <div class="qa-image-wrapper">
              <img
                src={assetPath('tos_exp.png')}
                alt="Probing internal spatial belief"
                class="qa-image"
                loading="lazy"
              />
            </div>
            <p class="qa-caption">Exploration history is converted into a probed cognitive map and updated over time.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  

</section> 

<script>
  // Format numbers to show 'k' for thousands
  function formatNumber(num: number) {
    return num > 999 ? (num / 1000).toFixed(1) + 'k' : num;
  }

  // Fetch GitHub stats directly
  async function fetchGitHubStats() {
    try {
      // Try to use default numbers immediately
      const starsElement = document.getElementById('hero-stars-count');
      const forksElement = document.getElementById('hero-forks-count');
      
      // Fetch latest stats from GitHub
      const response = await fetch('https://api.github.com/repos/williamzhangNU/VAGEN');
      const data = await response.json();
      
      const stars = data.stargazers_count;
      const forks = data.forks_count;
      
      // Update stars count if available and different from default
      if (starsElement && stars !== undefined) {
        const formattedStars = formatNumber(stars);
        if (formattedStars !== '1.4k') {
          starsElement.textContent = formattedStars;
        }
      }
      
      // Update forks count if available and different from default
      if (forksElement && forks !== undefined) {
        const formattedForks = formatNumber(forks);
        if (formattedForks !== '105') {
          forksElement.textContent = formattedForks;
        }
      }
    } catch (error) {
      console.error('Error fetching GitHub stats:', error);
      // Keep default values on error
    }
  }

  // Run when page is loaded
  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', fetchGitHubStats);
</script> 

<style>
  .cta-button {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 12px;
    padding: 12px 24px;
    background: linear-gradient(45deg, #2a2f6c, #4b4f8f);
    border: none;
    border-radius: 50px;
    color: white;
    font-size: 18px;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: all 0.3s ease;
    box-shadow: 0 4px 15px rgba(42, 47, 108, 0.2);
  }

  .cta-button:hover {
    transform: translateY(-2px);
    box-shadow: 0 6px 20px rgba(42, 47, 108, 0.3);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .button-icon {
    width: 24px;
    height: 24px;
    fill: currentColor;
  }

  .button-text {
